    history_html: `<p><strong>2015:</strong> World of Warships launches with cosmetic-focused monetization. Early loot systems are relatively straightforward with transparent odds.</p><p><strong>2017-2020:</strong> Premium ship containers are introduced with increasing frequency. The mixing of cosmetics and progression items increases monetization complexity.</p><p><strong>2021-2024:</strong> World of Warships continues aggressive container monetization with premium ships mixed with cosmetics. The system becomes increasingly predatory despite player complaints.</p>`,
    controversy_html: `<p>World of Warships faces similar criticism to World of Tanks for deliberately mixing cosmetics and progression items in loot boxes. The system creates pay-to-progress mechanics while maintaining veneer of cosmetic monetization. Players cannot determine whether opening containers provides cosmetic convenience or gameplay advantage.</p><p>The game has been criticized in community forums for lack of transparency about container contents and odds. Regulatory investigations into War Gaming's monetization practices continue in multiple regions.</p>`,
    tips_html: `<p><strong>Tip 1:</strong> Research community databases to identify which containers contain premium ships versus pure cosmetics before spending.</p><p><strong>Tip 2:</strong> Never expect to obtain specific premium ships from containers. The system is randomized with no pity.</p><p><strong>Tip 3:</strong> Accept that premium ship players have earning and progression advantages through spending. Focus on skill development instead.</p><p><strong>Tip 4:</strong> Consider whether World of Warships' predatory monetization is worth your time compared to alternative games.</p><p><strong>Tip 5:</strong> Demand transparent odds and clear cosmetic-versus-progression distinction from War Gaming.</p>`,
    editorial_html: `<p>World of Warships mirrors World of Tanks' problematic hybrid cosmetic-progression monetization. The system deliberately mixes cosmetics and premium ships in randomized containers, creating confusion about spending value. This intentional obfuscation is designed to maximize spending without player understanding.</p><p>The semi-pay-to-progress nature makes World of Warships worse than purely cosmetic games but not as egregiously predatory as pure pay-to-win titles. The system disrespects player intelligence by intentionally obscuring whether spending provides cosmetics or gameplay advantage.</p><p>World of Warships should be approached cautiously. Free-to-play is possible but requires accepting disadvantages from premium ship spenders. The system should be condemned for its lack of transparency and hybrid pay-to-progress mechanics that harm competitive fairness.</p>`,
