    comparable_slugs: ["black-desert-online", "guild-wars-2", "maplestory"],
    overview_html: `<p><strong>Lost Ark</strong> features Crystalline Aura and Mari's Shop gacha systems where players spend Royal Crystals (premium currency) on randomized boxes containing cosmetics and progression items. The primary gacha system costs 1.99 USD per pull with a 25-pull guaranteed item system. Lost Ark's primary controversy is the mixing of cosmetics and progression items—some boxes contain character progression materials alongside cosmetics, creating pay-to-progress mechanics.</p><p>The 25-pull pity system is relatively generous compared to many competitors, but the inclusion of progression materials in loot boxes creates pay-to-progression dynamics that undermine free-to-play fairness. Cosmetics are cosmetic-only, but the progression items available through gacha boxes create pressure to spend beyond cosmetic convenience.</p>`,
    pity_explanation_html: `<p><strong>Lost Ark features a 25-pull pity system costing approximately $50 USD.</strong> This is among the most generous pity systems available, guaranteeing items after a relatively short pull count. However, the inclusion of progression materials in loot boxes means pity doesn't guarantee cosmetics—it guarantees either cosmetics or progression materials.</p><p>The pity system is player-friendly in terms of pull count, but the mixing of item types makes the system less clear. Players might reach pity and receive progression materials instead of cosmetics, feeling the guarantee was wasted.</p>`,
    history_html: `<p><strong>2019:</strong> Lost Ark launches in Korea with cosmetic-focused gacha system. Early implementation features relatively transparent odds and reasonable pity systems.</p><p><strong>2022:</strong> Lost Ark releases globally (North America and Europe) with Amazon Games publishing. Western release expands monetization with more aggressive gacha mechanics.</p><p><strong>2023-2024:</strong> Lost Ark continues expanding gacha systems with additional cosmetic tiers and progression boxes. The game becomes known for aggressive monetization despite 25-pull pity system.</p>`,
    controversy_html: `<p>Lost Ark faces criticism for the mixing of cosmetics and progression materials in the same gacha systems. While the 25-pull pity is generous, reaching pity might yield progression materials instead of cosmetics, creating disappointment. The game's messaging about cosmetic convenience versus progression advantage is deliberately unclear.</p><p>Western players have criticized the game's monetization as more aggressive than the Korean version. Amazon Games' publishing decisions have expanded cosmetic pricing and accelerated loot box release cycles, making the game feel increasingly pay-to-progress.</p>`,
    tips_html: `<p><strong>Tip 1:</strong> The 25-pull pity is generous, but expect to reach pity for progression materials rather than cosmetics. Don't count on pity exclusively for cosmetic acquisition.</p><p><strong>Tip 2:</strong> Lost Ark's cosmetics are expensive—set a monthly budget ($5-10) and stick to it strictly to avoid overspending.</p><p><strong>Tip 3:</strong> Progression materials in gacha boxes create pay-to-progress mechanics. Accept that spending players progress faster and focus on skill development instead.</p><p><strong>Tip 4:</strong> Wait for cosmetics to return in future events rather than chasing limited-edition items. Many items rotate back into availability.</p><p><strong>Tip 5:</strong> Consider whether Lost Ark's monetization philosophy matches your budget. Alternative MMOs might offer better value for cosmetics.</p>`,
    editorial_html: `<p>Lost Ark's 25-pull pity system is the game's strongest point, offering generous guarantees compared to many competitors. However, the mixing of cosmetics and progression materials in the same gacha system undermines this advantage. Reaching pity might yield progression items rather than cosmetics, making the guarantee less valuable than it initially appears.</p><p>The game's western release with Amazon Games feels more aggressive than the Korean version, with faster cosmetic release cycles and higher prices. This suggests localization decisions have prioritized revenue over player experience.</p><p>Lost Ark is moderately safe for cosmetic spending thanks to the 25-pull pity, but budget carefully. The mixing of item types in gacha boxes creates potential disappointment, and progression materials in cosmetic systems feel predatory. Play for free or spend sparingly on cosmetics you genuinely love.</p>`,
