    overview_html: `<p><strong>EVE Online</strong> features cosmetic loot boxes called "skins" that cost 19.99 USD per pull, containing random cosmetics for ships and characters. The system is extremely expensive compared to other games, with cosmetics that might cost $20 in other games costing $19.99 per gamble. However, EVE's primary monetization advantage is that cosmetics are purely cosmetic—no gameplay advantages are purchasable through loot boxes.</p><p>The expensive pull cost combined with no pity system makes individual cosmetics extremely expensive to obtain. Players frequently spend $100+ without obtaining desired skins. However, the guarantee that cosmetics never affect gameplay preserves competitive integrity for free-to-play players. The system is expensive but fair in terms of preventing pay-to-win scenarios.</p>`,
    pity_explanation_html: `<p><strong>EVE Online has no pity system for cosmetic boxes.</strong> With cosmetics costing $19.99 per pull and loot pools containing dozens of items, obtaining specific skins can cost $500+ without any guarantee of payoff. There is no safety net for cosmetic collectors, making the system purely gambling.</p><p>The lack of pity system combined with the high cost-per-pull makes EVE's cosmetic gambling among the most expensive in all gaming. Players should expect realistic costs of $100+ for any specific cosmetic.</p>`,
    history_html: `<p><strong>2011:</strong> EVE Online introduces cosmetic ship skins with direct purchase options. Initial monetization is transparent with no loot boxes.</p><p><strong>2016:</strong> Loot boxes are introduced as a cosmetic system alternative to direct purchases. The system offers randomization at a price discount compared to direct cosmetics.</p><p><strong>2017-2024:</strong> EVE continues cosmetic-only loot boxes with no gameplay advantage. The cosmetic approach is maintained despite expensive pull costs.</p>`,
    controversy_html: `<p>EVE Online's cosmetic loot boxes face minimal criticism regarding fairness because cosmetics are entirely cosmetic. However, the $19.99 per pull cost is among the highest in gaming, making cosmetics extraordinarily expensive to collect. Players criticize the pricing as aggressive, though they acknowledge the cosmetic-only philosophy.</p><p>The primary criticism is that direct cosmetic purchases might offer better value, making loot boxes a poor choice. Community debate about whether players should support loot boxes versus direct cosmetics continues.</p>`,
    tips_html: `<p><strong>Tip 1:</strong> EVE cosmetics are extraordinarily expensive—never spend on loot boxes unless willing to lose $100+ searching for a specific skin.</p><p><strong>Tip 2:</strong> Direct cosmetic purchases are often cheaper than loot box gambling. Check the EVE store for direct skin options before opening boxes.</p><p><strong>Tip 3:</strong> Accept that obtaining rare cosmetics requires either tremendous luck or spending $500+. Focus on affordable skins instead.</p><p><strong>Tip 4:</strong> EVE's cosmetic-only system preserves competitive integrity—free players are never mechanically disadvantaged by refusing to spend.</p><p><strong>Tip 5:</strong> Budget cosmetics carefully at extreme price points. A single cosmetic might cost as much as an entire AAA game.</p>`,
    editorial_html: `<p>EVE Online's cosmetic-only loot boxes are fundamentally fair in terms of gameplay preservation, but the $19.99 per pull cost makes the system extraordinarily expensive. Obtaining specific cosmetics frequently costs $100+, making this among the most expensive cosmetic systems in all gaming.</p><p>The lack of pity system combined with the high cost-per-pull creates situations where cosmetic collectors can spend unlimited money without obtaining desired items. However, the cosmetic-only philosophy prevents this from being pay-to-win, which is the critical difference from predatory games.</p><p>EVE Online is safe from pay-to-win but unsafe from financial exploitation. Budget cosmetics extremely carefully and consider whether the cosmetics are worth the astronomical costs. The game respects competitive integrity while disrespecting wallet integrity.</p>`,
