    overview_html: `<p><strong>Call of Duty: Mobile</strong> features weapon cosmetic gacha through draw systems costing 1.99 USD per pull, containing random weapon blueprints and character cosmetics. The system offers no pity guarantees and features massive loot pools (100+ items per draw), making specific cosmetics extraordinarily expensive. The game distinguishes between cosmetics (weapon skins, characters) and gameplay items, keeping cosmetics purely cosmetic.</p><p>However, some weapon blueprints are locked behind gacha, creating FOMO around exclusive weapon appearances that eventually become meta-relevant. The lack of pity system combined with large loot pools makes obtaining specific blueprints extremely expensive—players frequently spend $50+ without obtaining their desired skin.</p>`,
    pity_explanation_html: `<p><strong>Call of Duty: Mobile has no pity system.</strong> With draws containing 100+ cosmetics and no guaranteed rarity progression, players have no safety net when gambling for cosmetics. Individual item odds are frequently under 1%, making obtaining specific items extremely unlikely without substantial spending.</p><p>The absence of any pity system combined with massive loot pools makes this among the worst cosmetic systems in mobile gaming. Players cannot plan spending because there's no guaranteed endpoint.</p>`,
    history_html: `<p><strong>2019:</strong> Call of Duty: Mobile launches with cosmetic draw systems. Early implementation features reasonable loot pool sizes and competitive odds.</p><p><strong>2020-2021:</strong> Draw loot pools expand significantly with new weapon cosmetics and character skins. Individual item odds decline as pool sizes grow.</p><p><strong>2022-2024:</strong> Call of Duty: Mobile continues aggressive draw monetization with collaboration events featuring licensed cosmetics. Loot pool sizes reach 100+ items per draw.</p>`,
    controversy_html: `<p>Call of Duty: Mobile faces criticism for cosmetic loot pools that have ballooned to 100+ items per draw, making specific cosmetics unrealistic to obtain without spending $100+. The lack of pity system combined with growing pool sizes makes the monetization feel increasingly predatory.</p><p>The game has been criticized for collaborating with popular IP (anime, superhero) to create FOMO around limited-edition cosmetics. These collaborations are designed to exploit fan psychology and extract maximum spending.</p>`,
    tips_html: `<p><strong>Tip 1:</strong> Never target specific weapon blueprints in large draws. With 100+ items per draw, your odds are likely under 1%.</p><p><strong>Tip 2:</strong> Call of Duty: Mobile's cosmetics are purely cosmetic—competitive advantage requires skill, not spending. Accept not obtaining every cosmetic.</p><p><strong>Tip 3:</strong> Set a monthly draw budget ($5-10) and treat it as entertainment expense. Never expect to obtain specific cosmetics.</p><p><strong>Tip 4:</strong> Wait for cosmetics to return in future events or stores. Most weapon blueprints eventually rotate back into availability.</p><p><strong>Tip 5:</strong> Seasonal battle pass cosmetics often offer better value than gacha draws. Prioritize battle pass over draw spending.</p>`,
    editorial_html: `<p>Call of Duty: Mobile's weapon cosmetic draws are problematic primarily because massive loot pool sizes (100+ items) make individual cosmetics effectively unrealistic to obtain. The lack of pity system combined with large pools means players have no guaranteed endpoint for spending.</p><p>The addition of licensed IP cosmetics from anime and superhero franchises exploits collector psychology and creates artificial FOMO around cosmetics that provide zero gameplay advantage. Players spend irrationally for cosmetics solely due to psychological attachment to external IP.</p><p>Call of Duty: Mobile should be played with strict cosmetic budgets. The game respects competitive integrity by keeping cosmetics purely cosmetic, but disrespects wallet integrity through predatory draw mechanics. Budget $10 per month maximum and accept you'll rarely obtain desired blueprints.</p>`,
